ng forward in this thread  but what she said really struck me so  she said just because we don't believe  in  thin ideal should we be using our  clients or patients as martyrs for the  cause and the other thing she said which  I thought was very important and  interesting was that you know because a  lot of dietitians struggle with eating  disorders in their past we come from a  lot of disordered eating backgrounds  could we be projecting our own recovery  on our clients or patients and that  really hit me hard when I was thinking  about what she said because I never want  to do anything that doesn't help the  public I never want to be pushing my own  agenda at somebody else's expense but I  also realize that you know I'm coming at  this from my own experience you know my  damaging experience with diet culture  and I know that's a very common  experience like you guys are telling me  every single day in the comments section  how common this is but I also have to  realize that I may come off as  insensitive to some of you who may have  alternative experiences that I cannot  personally relate to and therefore it  might seem like I kind of gloss over  them now this article and the the  Facebook thread that I'm referring to  really sparked some amazing  conversations about the obesity  treatment versus the haze dietitian  world and you know those things really  got me feeling a bit lonely that I  didn't feel confident taking you know  one hard stance in this debate I know  I've said this in a previous video where  I mentioned that I don't identify myself  as a hazed dietician but rather that I  am kind of a hazed learner I know this  is controversial so I will just say this  I am a hey supporter and a hazed learner  I'm not a hazed expert that's still very  true in fact if in case you're wondering  a lot of haze dietitians don't like me  because they feel I'm too moderate in my  kind of stance when it comes to dieting  and weight loss like I haven't gone all  in so to speak so I've been operating in  this awkward gray space with you know a  true appreciation for the haze movement  and the philosophy and I've been letting  it inform my work  however having said all that I  think queer the disconnect lies for for  you guys is that I haven't been loud  enough about this gray space I haven't  been explicit enough about what it feels  like for me or even for you honestly I  think I've just gotten so caught up in  the social justice aspect and the  political correctness of the non-diet  movement and the fact that I just so  badly want society to change that I feel  like I've kind of lost touch with my  true purpose as a registered dietitian  and that is ultimately to help improve  individuals well-being and health both  physical and emotional health honestly I  am just one person so even though I so  badly want to I just feel ill equipped  to ultimately change society at large so  for a lot of people of course like  focusing on improving your health starts  with intuitive eating and accepting and  loving your body exactly as it is but  what about for those people who  weight-loss would improve their health  either physically or emotionally some  are these in the Hayes community would  say unequivocally that this is not a  thing that weight loss is not needed  that we need to just focus on body  acceptance now there are some more kind  of moderate Hayes dietitians that I've  liaised with who acknowledge that yes  like weight loss may help improve some  risk factors for disease and improve  health status however dieting we know  doesn't work so recommending dieting is  in a sense an irresponsible thing for us  to do as registered dieticians because  we know that it can actually be you know  kind of useless at best and really  dangerous at worse  to your physical and metabolic and  emotional health I believe that I've  seen so much of that and I talked about  that in my previous video here and  here's my professional research informed  opinion weight loss may improve certain  chronic health conditions there is a lot  of correlational research linking weight  loss and improved health outcomes and in  some cases I can't deny that it may play  a role the challenge with the  recommendation of course to just go lose  weight  is that the research suggests time and  time again that weight loss diets do not  work so some might say that going on in  intentional weight loss diet is a bit of  a risk and benefit analysis and that's  only something that you can decide  whether it's worth doing with your  practitioner and a registered dietician  but I still don't feel fully satisfied  that that is enough for some of you and  I'm not sure that it's enough for me  either  we know that diets don't work I mean the  research tells us that but does that  mean that we should stop looking for  solutions that do my heart says no so  what I really want to reiterate in this  quick little vlog video here is that I  truly believe in client centered care  I believe in body autonomy I believe  that you have a right to want to change  your body even if that means it  reinforces diet culture and even if I  don't think as a professional that it's  gonna have a measurable impact on your  health you have that right and I support  that right this like isn't a new belief  this is not something that I just came  up with right now this is something I've  talked about before however I do think  on you know further reflection that I've  been maybe unnecessarily critical of  youtubers who who promote weight loss or  promote fat loss and I've done that in  my effort to call out how insidious diet  culture really is but through a little  bit of reflection I think I've come to  terms with the idea that I think I need  to be a little bit more neutral in my  analysis in an effort to stop the shame  that people feel around wanting to lose  weight I have always said that it's  completely normal to want to lose weight  we live and breathe India culture that  is a totally normal thing to want and I  support a wide range of goals so you  know I've come to realize like Who am I  to say that you know wanting to lose  weight for health reasons is okay but  it's not for aesthetic reasons I just  realized that that is not fair for me to  decide and you know I could keep  hammering away at this despite feeling  this kind of icky disconnect but I think  part of being a  good strong professional means  continuing to learn and to gather  information and to apply those learnings  to your practice being Switzerland is  never sexy but I think what's most  important here is that we're  evidence-based and we're client centered  bottom line if you want to give up the  pursuit of weight loss I support you if  you want to continue to try to lose  weight I support you honestly I would  rather you get evidence-based  information from registered dietitians  like me than to go follow a dangerous  meal plan from a you know wellness  youtuber online so to close here folks I  want to just hammer home that if I have  ever lost sight of your needs or made  you feel unheard in your experiences or  your desire to lose weight I'm sorry I'm  truly truly deeply sorry I am going to  personally work to change that so that  abby's kitchen is a supportive space for  inspiring everyone's unique version of  health so tell me what it is that you  all need from me and I will do my  absolute best to make it happen so on  that note definitely leave me some  comments below give this video a like if  you like this message and if this is  helpful for you I hope that you're  feeling supportive I hope that you know  that I am with you on your journey of  course subscribe to the channel and I  will see you next time on Abby's kitchen  bye  [Music]  [Music]

